Pull vs Push Model in Monitoring

🔹 What is it? 🔄
 In monitoring systems, data collection happens in two ways:
 👉 Pull Model and Push Model
Understanding this is key to how tools like Prometheus work.

🔹 Why it matters in real-world? 🏗️
 The way metrics are collected impacts:
 ✔ Reliability
 ✔ Scalability
 ✔ Debugging
👉 Choosing the right model = better monitoring system 🚀

🔹 Pull Model Explained: 📥
✔ Monitoring system requests (pulls) data from targets
 ✔ Targets expose metrics via endpoints
👉 Example:
 Prometheus → sends request → gets metrics
✔ Advantages:
Better control over data collection
Easy to detect if target is down
Works well in dynamic systems

🔹 Push Model Explained: 📤
✔ Applications send (push) data to monitoring system
👉 Example:
 App → sends metrics → monitoring tool
✔ Advantages:
Useful for short-lived jobs
No need for scraping endpoints
✔ Limitation:
Harder to track failed systems
Can lead to data inconsistency

🔹 Why Prometheus Uses Pull Model? 💡
👉 Prometheus follows pull model by design because:
✔ Can check if target is alive (health check)
 ✔ Centralized control of scraping
 ✔ Better suited for Kubernetes & microservices
 ✔ Avoids duplicate or inconsistent data

🔹 Real-world Example: 🌍
👉 Pull Model:
 Prometheus checks server every 15s
 If no response → server is down 🚨
👉 Push Model:
 Server sends data
 If server crashes → no data sent ❌ (hard to detect issue)

🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ Pull = Prometheus collects 📥
 ✔ Push = App sends 📤
 ✔ Pull = More reliable 🔄
 ✔ Push = Useful for short jobs
